# Coding Standards & Best Practices

Coding standards for Laravel + Vue 3 applications with PHP 8.2+ and TypeScript.

## Code Quality Principles

### 1. Readability First

- Code is read more than written
- Clear variable and function names
- Self-documenting code preferred over comments
- Consistent formatting

### 2. KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid)

- Simplest solution that works
- Avoid over-engineering
- No premature optimization
- Easy to understand > clever code

### 3. DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)

- Extract common logic into functions
- Create reusable components
- Share utilities across modules
- Avoid copy-paste programming

### 4. YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It)

- Don't build features before they're needed
- Avoid speculative generality
- Add complexity only when required
- Start simple, refactor when needed

## PHP/Laravel Standards

### Variable Naming (PHP)

```php
// ✅ GOOD: Descriptive names with proper casing
$marketSearchQuery = 'election';
$isUserAuthenticated = true;
$totalRevenue = 1000;

// ❌ BAD: Unclear names
$q = 'election';
$flag = true;
$x = 1000;
```

### Function/Method Naming (PHP)

```php
// ✅ GOOD: Clear, descriptive method names
public function fetchMarketData(int $marketId): Market { }
public function calculateProbability(Market $market, Option $option): float { }
public function isValidEmail(string $email): bool { }

// ❌ BAD: Unclear names
public function market($id) { }
public function calculate($m, $o) { }
public function check($e) { }
```

### Type Declarations (PHP 8.2+)

```php
// ✅ GOOD: Full type declarations
public function calculateTotal(array $items): float
{
    return array_sum(array_column($items, 'price'));
}

public function getUserById(int $id): ?User
{
    return User::find($id);
}

// ❌ BAD: No type hints
public function calculateTotal($items)
{
    return array_sum(array_column($items, 'price'));
}
```

### Laravel Controller Standards

```php
// ✅ GOOD: Thin controller using services
class MarketController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct(
        protected MarketService $marketService
    ) {}

    public function store(StoreMarketRequest $request): RedirectResponse
    {
        $market = $this->marketService->create($request->validated());

        return redirect()
            ->route('markets.show', $market)
            ->with('message', 'Market created successfully');
    }
}

// ❌ BAD: Fat controller with business logic
class MarketController extends Controller
{
    public function store(Request $request)
    {
        // 100 lines of validation, logic, database operations
        // All in the controller - BAD!
    }
}
```

### Laravel Service Layer

```php
// ✅ GOOD: Service with single responsibility
class MarketService
{
    public function __construct(
        protected MarketRepositoryInterface $repository
    ) {}

    /**
     * Create a new market with options.
     *
     * @param array $data Market data including options
     * @return Market The created market
     * @throws \Exception If creation fails
     */
    public function create(array $data): Market
    {
        return DB::transaction(function () use ($data) {
            $market = $this->repository->create([
                'name' => $data['name'],
                'description' => $data['description'],
                'category' => $data['category'],
                'creator_id' => auth()->id(),
            ]);

            foreach ($data['options'] as $option) {
                $market->options()->create($option);
            }

            event(new MarketCreated($market));

            return $market->load('options');
        });
    }
}
```

### Laravel Form Request Validation

